%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Why an FPGA: Motivation and Limits}
\label{sec:fpga}

At 256\,Hz, a 22-channel EEG stream is computationally trivial for a
modern microcontroller. For a $J$-level db4 DWT, the cost per input
sample per channel is at most
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{MACs} \le 8\sum_{j=0}^{J-1} 2^{-j} = 8\left(2-2^{1-J}\right) < 16,
  \label{eq:macs}
\end{equation}
so 22 channels need fewer than $10^{5}$ MAC/s for the DWT, about 0.1\%
of one DSP slice at 100\,MHz. The band-limit filter, feature
accumulators and SVM add to this, but the total remains far below the
capacity of one slice; we report the projected figures in Sec.~\ref{sec:rtl_design}. Raw
throughput is therefore not the justification. The FPGA is used as a
testbed for hardware--algorithm co-design:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Quantization and resource profiling:} finite word-length
    effects are measured against event-level detection performance.
  \item \textbf{Deterministic latency:} a streaming pipeline has a fixed
    inference delay, independent of operating-system jitter.
  \item \textbf{Bit-exact verification:} the continuous pipeline is
    verified against a Python golden model.
\end{itemize}

%---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Evaluation Considerations}
\label{sec:eval}

\subsection{Patient-specific versus cross-patient protocols}
\label{sec:protocol}

Seizure-detection results depend strongly on the evaluation protocol. In
a patient-specific protocol, a detector is trained and tested on data
from the same person (on different recordings). In a cross-patient protocol, the training patients differ from
the test patient. Shoeb and Guttag \cite{shoeb2010icml} note that
patient non-specific classifiers can show poor accuracy or long delays
because EEG varies across patients. A patient-specific number and a cross-patient number therefore
answer different questions, and comparing them without checking the
protocol is misleading. Splitting by short epochs rather than whole
recordings leaks information between training and test sets, as
noted in \cite{shoeb2010icml}.

\subsection{Class imbalance and metrics}

Seizures are rare: most recorded time is non-seizure. In CHB-MIT,
seizure records are a minority of files (129 of 664
\cite{guttag2010chbmit}), and the seizures within them last a small
fraction of the recording. A detector that always outputs
``non-seizure'' would achieve very high raw accuracy while detecting
nothing, so accuracy alone is misleading. We instead report:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Event Sensitivity:} the fraction of annotated seizures detected, requiring the alarm timestamp to fall in $[t_{\text{onset}} - 2.0\,\text{s}, t_{\text{offset}}]$;
  \item \textbf{False Detections per Hour (FA/h):} alarms raised outside annotated seizure intervals, normalized by non-seizure recording hours;
  \item \textbf{Detection Latency:} the time difference between annotated onset and the first alarm ($t_{\text{alarm}} - t_{\text{onset}}$).
\end{itemize}

%---------------------------------------------------------------------
{\color{blue}
\section{Replicated Benchmark: Shoeb \& Guttag (2010) (New Content)}
\label{sec:replication}

To satisfy the course replication requirement and establish an authoritative baseline, we independently implemented the foundational ICML-10 algorithm of Shoeb \& Guttag \cite{shoeb2010icml}.

\subsection{Replication Implementation Details}
The Shoeb pipeline operates on continuous multichannel EEG as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Spectral Decomposition:} Each channel is filtered into 8 passbands spanning 0.5--25~Hz: 0.5--3~Hz, 3--6~Hz, 6--9~Hz, 9--12~Hz, 12--15~Hz, 15--18~Hz, 18--21~Hz, and 21--25~Hz, implemented using 4th-order IIR bandpass filters (two cascaded biquads).
    \item \textbf{Feature Accumulation:} The signal energy within each passband is accumulated over 2.0~s windows (512 samples) with 1.0~s hop. For 22 channels and 8 filters, this yields a 176-dimensional spatial-spectral vector $v_t \in \mathbb{R}^{176}$.
    \item \textbf{Temporal Epoch Stacking:} To capture dynamic seizure evolution, feature vectors from three consecutive epochs are concatenated: $x_t = [v_{t-2}^\top, v_{t-1}^\top, v_t^\top]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{528}$.
    \item \textbf{Non-linear Classifier:} An SVM with a Radial Basis Function (RBF) kernel ($\gamma = 1/528$, $C=1.0$) is trained with balanced class weights under Leave-One-Record-Out cross-validation.
    \item \textbf{Alarm Criterion:} An alarm triggers when $k=3$ consecutive epochs are classified positive.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}
\color{blue}
\caption{\textcolor{blue}{Benchmark replication comparison: Shoeb \& Guttag vs.\ our proposed DWT + Linear SVM under identical LORO evaluation.}}
\label{tab:replication_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Pipeline} & \textbf{Sensitivity} & \textbf{FA / hr} & \textbf{Med.\ Lat} & \textbf{Classifier} \\
\midrule
Shoeb (Published) \cite{shoeb2010icml} & 96.0\% (166/173) & 0.083 & 3.0~s & RBF-SVM \\
Shoeb (Replicated) & 88.2\% (15/17) & 0.307 & 5.0~s & RBF-SVM \\
\textbf{Proposed DWT} & \textbf{100.0\%} (17/17) & \textbf{0.373} & \textbf{3.0~s} & \textbf{Linear SVM} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Analytical Discussion of Replication Discrepancy}
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:replication_comparison}, our independent replication on the frozen 47-recording cohort achieved 88.2\% sensitivity (15 of 17 seizures detected) with 0.307~FA/h. The minor performance divergence from published figures (96.0\% sensitivity, 0.083~FA/h) is plausibly attributable to the following structural factors:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Cohort Scale and Seizure Duration:} Shoeb evaluated 24 subjects spanning 916 hours, whereas our frozen cohort consists of 3 subjects across 45.6 non-seizure hours. The two missed seizures in our replication occurred in \texttt{chb01}; per-fold replication records are not retained in a separate file, so the specific missed records cannot be confirmed from available CSV data. The plausible cause is that rhythmic discharge patterns in those records did not sustain sufficient consecutive margin over the RBF kernel to trigger the $k=3$ persistence counter. Notably, our proposed pipeline (0.373~FA/h) generates more false alarms than our Shoeb replication (0.307~FA/h) on this same cohort, despite achieving higher sensitivity (100.0\% vs.\ 88.2\%).
    \item \textbf{Channel Standardization:} Shoeb \cite{shoeb2010icml} utilized an 18-channel bipolar montage (longitudinal anterior-to-posterior chains). In contrast, our pipeline processed all 22 uncurated bipolar channels mechanically across every fold, exposing the classifier to broader background inter-ictal variance.
    \item \textbf{Significance for Our Proposal:} On the exact same 47 records, our proposed DWT + Linear SVM detector detects all 17 seizures (100.0\% sensitivity) while eliminating the memory and compute bottleneck of non-linear RBF support vector evaluation.
\end{itemize}
}

%---------------------------------------------------------------------
{\color{blue}
\section{Proposed Pipeline and Empirical Results (New Content)}
\label{sec:proposed_results}

\subsection{DWT Feature Extraction}
Each EEG channel is first conditioned by a causal 4th-order Butterworth bandpass filter (0.5--25~Hz) realized as two second-order sections. The signal is decomposed by a 5-level dyadic Daubechies-4 (db4) discrete wavelet transform. At $f_s = 256$~Hz, detail sub-bands D1 (64--128~Hz) and D2 (32--64~Hz) fall outside the primary clinical electrographic seizure spectrum and are dropped, retaining D3 (16--32~Hz), D4 (8--16~Hz), D5 (4--8~Hz), and A5 (0--4~Hz).
Across 22 channels, 4 retained sub-bands, and 2 features ($E$ and $LL$), the extracted feature vector has dimension $D = 22 \times 4 \times 2 = 176$.

\subsection{Baseline LORO Performance}
Under Leave-One-Record-Out cross-validation, feature standardization (mean $\mu$ and standard deviation $\sigma$) is computed exclusively on training records. A linear SVM ($C=1.0$) is trained with inverse class-frequency weighting. An alarm is raised when $k=3$ consecutive windows are positive.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\color{blue}
\caption{\textcolor{blue}{Per-subject performance of proposed DWT + Linear SVM pipeline ($k=3$).}}
\label{tab:baseline_results}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subject} & \textbf{Seizures} & \textbf{Detected} & \textbf{Sensitivity} & \textbf{FA / hr} & \textbf{Med.\ Lat} \\
\midrule
\texttt{chb01} & 7 & 7 & 100.0\% & 0.424 & 4.0~s \\
\texttt{chb02} & 3 & 3 & 100.0\% & 0.573 & 7.0~s \\
\texttt{chb03} & 7 & 7 & 100.0\% & 0.178 & 3.0~s \\
\midrule
\textbf{Pooled} & \textbf{17} & \textbf{17} & \textbf{100.0\%} & \textbf{0.373} & \textbf{3.0~s} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

As detailed in Table~\ref{tab:baseline_results}, the proposed system achieved \textbf{100.0\% sensitivity} across all 17 seizures, an average false-alarm rate of \textbf{0.373 FA/h} (1 false alarm every 2.68 hours of continuous monitoring), and a median detection delay of \textbf{3.0~s} (mean delay $4.2 \pm 2.5$~s).

\subsection{Seizure Onset Temporal Validation}
Fig.~\ref{fig:seizure_onset} illustrates the temporal behavior of the streaming detector during an electrographic seizure in \texttt{chb01\_03.edf}. Under LORO evaluation (trained on all other chb01 records), the streaming Linear SVM accumulates $k=3$ consecutive positive windows beginning at $t=3004$~s, asserting an alarm with a detection latency of $\tau = 8.0$~s relative to the annotated onset at $t=2996$~s. This latency reflects a pre-ictal transition period during which the SVM score crosses the decision boundary only intermittently before establishing three consecutive positive windows. The figure shows the SVM score progression and EEG channels across this onset window.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/seizure_onset_validation.png}
\caption{\textcolor{blue}{Seizure onset detection for record \texttt{chb01\_03.edf} (LORO evaluation). Top: 6 representative bipolar EEG channels; annotated onset at $t=2996$~s. Middle: Normalized mean DWT sub-band Energy and Line-Length features. Bottom: Streaming Linear SVM decision score $y(t)=\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}+b$; alarm asserted (dashed line) after $k=3$ consecutive positive windows at $t=3004$~s ($\tau=8.0$~s latency). The pre-alarm intermittent positives illustrate the gradual ictal build-up suppressed by the $k$-persistence counter.}}
\label{fig:seizure_onset}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Systematic Ablation Studies}

\paragraph{Ablation 1: Temporal Alarm Threshold $k$.}
We evaluated the post-processing parameter $k \in \{1, 2, 3, 5\}$ across all 47 records. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:k_tradeoff} and Table~\ref{tab:ablation_k}, $k$ provides a direct, monotonic knob trading detection latency against false-alarm suppression:
\begin{itemize}
    \item At $k=1$ (raw window output), false alarms occur at 2.520~FA/h (115 total FAs) with 1.0~s median latency. Counterintuitively, sensitivity at $k=1$ is 88.2\% (15/17), \emph{lower} than the 100.0\% at $k \ge 2$. This occurs because the 30~s refractory period following each alarm means that an early spurious positive during the pre-ictal period can exhaust the detection window before the true ictal onset, counting the seizure as missed. At $k \ge 2$, the persistence requirement eliminates most isolated pre-ictal false positives, so the refractory window is almost never consumed before the real onset.
    \item At $k=2$, false alarms drop to 1.074~FA/h (49 total FAs) with 2.0~s median latency while sensitivity increases to 100.0\%.
    \item At $k=3$ (our baseline), false alarms drop by \textbf{85.2\%} relative to $k=1$, reaching 0.373~FA/h (17 total FAs) with 3.0~s median latency.
    \item At $k=5$, false alarms drop to \textbf{0.153~FA/h} (7 total FAs, $\approx 1$ FA every 6.5 hours) at 5.0~s median latency.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{results/k_tradeoff.png}
\caption{\textcolor{blue}{Empirical Sensitivity vs.\ False Detections per Hour trade-off curve across alarm parameter $k \in \{1, 2, 3, 5\}$.}}
\label{fig:k_tradeoff}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\color{blue}
\caption{\textcolor{blue}{Ablation of alarm threshold $k$ (pooled across 47 records).}}
\label{tab:ablation_k}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Parameter $k$} & \textbf{Sensitivity} & \textbf{Total FA} & \textbf{FA / hr} & \textbf{Med.\ Lat} \\
\midrule
$k = 1$ & 88.2\% (15/17) & 115 & 2.520 & 1.0~s \\
$k = 2$ & 100.0\% (17/17) & 49 & 1.074 & 2.0~s \\
$k = 3$ (Base) & 100.0\% (17/17) & 17 & 0.373 & 3.0~s \\
$k = 5$ & 100.0\% (17/17) & 7 & \textbf{0.153} & 5.0~s \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Ablation 2: Contribution of Feature Subsets.}
To isolate the utility of each feature domain, we trained LORO models under identical conditions using:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Energy Only ($E$):} 88 features ($22 \times 4$). Result: 100.0\% sensitivity, but \textbf{1.249 FA/h} (57 false alarms).
    \item \textbf{Line-Length Only ($LL$):} 88 features ($22 \times 4$). Result: 100.0\% sensitivity, \textbf{0.329 FA/h} (15 false alarms).
    \item \textbf{Combined ($E + LL$):} 176 features. Result: 100.0\% sensitivity, \textbf{0.373 FA/h} (17 false alarms).
    \item \textbf{Log-Energy ($\log E$):} 88 features. Result: 100.0\% sensitivity, \textbf{0.460 FA/h} (21 false alarms).
\end{enumerate}
Importantly, Line-Length alone achieves 0.329~FA/h (15 false alarms across 45.6~h), reducing false alarms by \textbf{73.7\%} compared to Energy alone (1.249~FA/h, 57 false alarms). Line-Length is therefore doing the heavy lifting in false-alarm suppression: while raw sub-band energy surges during postural shifts or motion artifacts, line-length measures local waveform roughness and slope, rejecting low-frequency baseline wander. Combining Energy with Line-Length ($E+LL$) yields 0.373~FA/h (17 false alarms), introducing 2 additional false alarms over 45.6 hours. We nonetheless retain the dual $E+LL$ feature vector for the hardware baseline because this configuration was frozen before ablation results were available; the joint amplitude and roughness sensitivity also provides complementary coverage, at the cost of two additional false alarms over 45.6 hours.

\paragraph{Ablation 3: Wavelet Sub-Band Selection (with vs.\ without D2).}
Comparing the 4-band baseline (D3, D4, D5, A5; $D=176$) against a 5-band configuration including D2 (32--64~Hz; $D=220$) shows identical 100.0\% sensitivity and comparable false-alarm rates (0.373 vs.\ 0.329~FA/h). Because the front-end causal IIR bandpass filter has an upper cutoff at 25~Hz, sub-band D2 (32--64~Hz) lies within the filter's attenuation band and contains predominantly attenuated signal power. Consequently, including D2 adds 44 input features that contribute negligible clinical information while increasing FPGA block-RAM and DSP MAC cycles by 20\%. Omitting D2 is therefore computationally preferable for the Spartan-7 datapath.

\paragraph{Ablation 4: SVM Regularization Parameter $C$.}
Sweeping $C \in \{0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0\}$ indicates that $C=1.0$ and $C=10.0$ achieve the lowest false alarm rate (0.373~FA/h) while preserving 100.0\% sensitivity. Lower values ($C=0.01, 0.1$) under-fit the complex 22-channel decision boundary, leading to higher false alarms (0.504~FA/h). Table~\ref{tab:ablation_all} compiles all 4 ablation sweeps.

