%---------------------------------------------------------------------
{\color{blue}
\section{Hardware Design and RTL Implementation (New Content)}
\label{sec:rtl_design}

\subsection{Data Throughput and UART Rate}
At $f_s = 256$~Hz, streaming 22 channels with 16-bit samples requires:
\begin{equation}
22 \times 256 = 5{,}632 \text{ samples/s} = 11{,}264 \text{ bytes/s}.
\end{equation}
With standard 8N1 serial framing (1 start, 8 data, 1 stop bit = 10 bits/byte), the raw bit rate is $11{,}264 \times 10 = 112{,}640$~baud. While 115,200 baud is theoretically sufficient, it leaves only a 2.2\% timing margin ($(115{,}200 - 112{,}640)/115{,}200 = 2.2\%$), leaving virtually zero tolerance for packet framing overhead, clock drift, or transmission jitter. We therefore configured the UART receiver at \textbf{230,400 baud}, providing over 51\% throughput headroom. At a 100~MHz system clock, each UART bit period spans exactly 434 clock cycles.

\subsection{Synthesizable Verilog RTL Modules}
We designed four synthesizable Verilog RTL modules accompanied by cycle-accurate verification testbenches located in \texttt{rtl/}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{rtl/dwt\_db4.v}: Single-level Daubechies-4 analysis filter bank building block. Implements the 8-tap low-pass ($h$) and high-pass ($g$) filters using signed 16-bit Q1.15 coefficients and a 36-bit accumulator. An internal phase toggle achieves decimation-by-2, outputting valid approximation and detail samples every two input clocks. Verified via \texttt{rtl/tb\_dwt\_db4.v} against software golden hex vectors (\texttt{vectors/tb\_dwt\_*.hex}).
    \item \texttt{rtl/svm\_classifier.v}: Pipelined Linear SVM decision engine. Performs the inner product $\mathbf{w}^\top \mathbf{x} + b$ over 176 clock cycles using a single time-multiplexed DSP48E1 multiplier. Weights and normalization scale factors are stored in block RAM. Verified via \texttt{rtl/tb\_svm\_classifier.v}.
    \item \texttt{rtl/alarm\_logic.v}: Temporal post-processing FSM. Tracks consecutive positive window classifications, asserting an alarm pulse and latching an alarm LED when the counter reaches $k=3$. Verified via \texttt{rtl/tb\_alarm\_logic.v}.
    \item \texttt{rtl/uart\_rx.v}: 230,400-baud UART receiver with double-flop metastability synchronization and byte-framing logic. Verified via \texttt{rtl/tb\_uart\_rx.v}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{FPGA Resource Projections (Spartan-7 XC7S50)}
Table~\ref{tab:resources} details the analytical hardware resource projections for the proposed real-time seizure detection pipeline targeting the Spartan-7 XC7S50 FPGA. These estimates are derived from datapath register widths and operational requirements:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{DSP48E1 Slices (2 allocated):} One DSP48E1 slice is time-multiplexed at 100~MHz to compute the 8-tap db4 DWT filter convolutions across 22 channels (5,632 sample/s throughput requires $<0.05\%$ of slice capacity). A second DSP48E1 slice executes the 176-cycle SVM dot product $\mathbf{w}^\top \mathbf{x} + b$ once per 1.0-second classification epoch.
    \item \textbf{Block RAM (4 blocks of 18~Kb):} Two 18~Kb BRAM primitives store circular history buffers for multi-channel sample shifting, and two primitives store fixed-point 16-bit SVM weights, channel scaling factors, and offsets.
    \item \textbf{Logic and Registers:} Slice LUTs (projected $\approx 1{,}450$, 4.45\%) and registers (projected $\approx 1{,}820$, 2.79\%) account for the 36-bit DWT accumulator, feature absolute-difference accumulators, UART shift registers, and the $k$-window alarm FSM.
\end{enumerate}
Full out-of-context logic synthesis, place-and-route, and bitstream generation targeting the RealDigital Boolean Board in Xilinx Vivado are scheduled for Stage 6 (Nov 1--5).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\color{blue}
\caption{\textcolor{blue}{Architectural resource projections on Spartan-7 XC7S50 (analytical datapath budget).}}
\label{tab:resources}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Resource} & \textbf{Pipeline Usage} & \textbf{Available (XC7S50)} & \textbf{Utilization} \\
\midrule
DSP48E1 Slices   & 2    & 120    & \textbf{1.67\%} \\
Block RAM (18Kb) & 4    & 150    & \textbf{2.67\%} \\
Slice LUTs       & 1450 & 32,600 & \textbf{4.45\%} \\
Slice Registers  & 1820 & 65,200 & \textbf{2.79\%} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Because the computational demand of the 5,632 sample/s pipeline is small relative to a 100~MHz clock, the entire system is projected to occupy less than 5\% of logic and memory resources, leaving ample headroom for UART buffering and on-chip diagnostic logic.
}

